## Code review (local, before push)

Review happens **locally before `git push`** — run a local code review with the built-in `/code-review` skill so PRs open already-reviewed and CI minutes aren't burned on a post-push review loop. A clean local review means the first CI run is also the last.

```bash
git fetch origin   # so origin/main is current before scoping the diff
# then run a local code review with the built-in /code-review skill against origin/main
```

- Triage: fix **Critical** + **Warning**; **Info** at discretion. Re-run once to confirm clean; cap at ~2 passes — don't grind.
- Compare against `origin/main`, not local `main` (local main is often stale, especially in a worktree — a stale base reviews every upstream commit you don't have).
- Invoke it deliberately — before the push that opens/updates the PR, not on every commit, and not from a git hook (a review takes minutes).
- **Persist findings for closeout.** After opening the PR, post the findings as one PR comment carrying the `<!-- local-review-findings -->` marker (each finding + how it was resolved, or why not; "0 findings" if clean). It is the durable record `/project-closeout` mines for recurring mistake families.
- For deeper passes the built-in `/security-review` command is also available. Skill: `<engsys-root>/skills/code-review/SKILL.md`.

## Pre-push gate

Local review first, then the project's pre-push check (`/pre-push`; the exact command is in Project facts) before **every** push. It inspects `git diff origin/main...HEAD`, runs the subset of CI gates relevant to the diff, and fails fast. **Do not push if it fails**; fix locally.

- Never `--no-verify` past a git hook — fix the violation. If the project documents an emergency bypass, anything it skipped (e.g. an E2E suite) is **not verified** until you see it green in CI before merge.
- Know what the gate does **not** cover (suites it never selects, deploy/CI matrices it doesn't simulate). "Pre-push passed" is not "CI will pass" for those — run them the way CI does or defer the verdict to CI and confirm before merge.
- Renamed/removed UI copy or a changed interaction flow on a tested screen breaks E2E selectors — grep the E2E specs for the old string before pushing.

## Git / PR conventions

- **Never** `git push origin main`. Direct main commits go via PR.
- **Open PRs as draft** (`gh pr create --draft`); mark Ready only after the local review is resolved and the pre-push gate is green — the ready transition often triggers the expensive CI matrix.
- Branches: `agent/<phase-or-project-slug>` for batches, `agent/<n>-<slug>` for single-issue work.
- Commit messages: `type(scope): subject (#issue)`. Types: `feat | fix | chore | docs | test | refactor | style | perf | build | ci | revert`. No `enh:` — use `feat:` for new behavior, `style:` for visual-only polish.
- Batch shape: one project phase = one branch/worktree/PR; a single issue = one PR. Each issue gets its own commit referencing `#issue`. Push **once**, after local validation — CI minutes cost money.
- PR closes syntax: **one keyword per line**. `Closes #1, #2` only closes #1 — use `Closes #1` then `Closes #2` on separate lines. If a comma-list already merged, close the rest by hand with a `Completed in PR #<pr>.` comment.
- Stacked branches: after each upstream merge, `git fetch origin && git rebase origin/main`; already-applied commits → `git rebase --skip` (not `--abort`); confirm with `git log --oneline origin/main..HEAD`; then `git push --force-with-lease origin <branch>`. **Never** `git push --force`.
- Secret scanning: if a pre-commit secret scanner is configured, install it locally. A verified false positive gets a narrow path/regex allowlist entry — never allowlist a real secret; rotate it.
- Lockfile conflicts: regenerate with the package manager and `git add` the result — never hand-merge a lockfile.
- Post-merge cleanup: `git checkout main && git pull --ff-only origin main`; `git worktree remove <path>` + `git worktree prune`; delete the merged local branch.
- Write PR/issue/commit bodies to a file under `tmp/` with the Write tool, then pass `--body-file`/`--file`. **No heredocs** — they mangle newlines.
- Commit/push only when the user asks. End commit messages with the `Co-Authored-By: Claude` trailer.

## Deferring work

Out-of-scope follow-ups found during a PR: **always** file a tracking issue — no silent deferrals, no "fix later" TODOs without a number.

1. Title cites the parent (e.g. "Follow-up to #123"); body has acceptance criteria + context and a `Deferred from #123` line; same labels as the parent.
2. Add it to the **same board/project as the parent**, in a sensible phase (issue-tracker skill `add-to-board`).

## Filing issues

1. **Investigate first** — search, root cause, real fix. No band-aids.
2. **Write the body to `tmp/issue-body-{slug}.md`** with the Write tool (no heredocs), starting from the matching issue template (bug / feature / enhancement / content / infrastructure).
3. **Create with `gh`**: `gh issue create --body-file tmp/issue-body-{slug}.md`. Labels: type + area — verify they exist first (`gh label list`).
4. Report back: issue number, URL, short RCA + fix summary, labels.

Quality over speed. Unsure whether it's worth an issue? Ask "file it or fix it?" — typos / one-liners, just fix.

## Tool preference order

Prefer the most direct, scriptable tool first; fall back to MCP, then manual:

| Need                          | First                            | Fallback                                    |
| ----------------------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| GitHub issues / PRs / Actions | `gh` CLI                         | `github` MCP (when `gh` auth/network fails) |
| GitHub ProjectV2 (boards)     | `gh project` / `gh api graphql`  | — MCP can't do ProjectV2                    |
| Browser automation (testing)  | `playwright` MCP                 | direct Playwright API                       |
| Browser debugging (UI)        | `chrome-devtools` MCP            | manual DevTools                             |

General rule: **gh CLI > MCP > manual/UI fallback.**

